{
 "id":"Q33",
 "sourceFile":"Position-Specific Differences in Speed Profiles Among National Football League Scouting Combine Participants.pdf",
 "decision":"INCLUDE",
 "exclusionReason":"",
 "degradedReason":"",
 "doiMissing":false,
 "identityCheck":"The title printed in the text, Position-Specific Differences in Speed Profiles Among National Football League Scouting Combine Participants (Gillen ZM), matches the filename and crossrefTitle.",
 "confidence":"high",
 "studyDesign":"Cross-sectional Study",
 "primaryDomain":"Monitoring & Technology",
 "domains":["Monitoring & Technology","Training & Performance"],
 "audiences":["Performance","Coaching","Research & Analytics"],
 "sports":["American Football"],
 "populations":["Professional / Elite"],
 "evidenceSummary":"This cross-sectional analysis used publicly available NFL Scouting Combine data from 2,156 American football players (2017, 2018, 2019, 2022 and 2023) with valid 40-yard dash and 10- and 20-yard split times. The authors calculated maximal velocity (vmax), acceleration time constant (t) and maximal acceleration (amax) from the splits, compared eight position groups with one-way ANOVA, and correlated these variables with other combine tests. Skill positions (DB and WR) had the highest vmax and amax and the lowest t, and offensive linemen had the lowest vmax and amax and the highest t.",
 "screeningLimitations":"The speed profiles are estimated from split times in a single testing setting, and the study has no training, injury or on-field data. The position differences are cross-sectional and cannot show what causes them, and the authors note that body size and position selection may explain part of the gap.",
 "synthesisCaution":"Staff could read the position gaps as fixed traits of each position, when the authors propose that selection and body size may account for much of the difference rather than training alone.",
 "paper":{
   "year":2024,
   "citation":"Gillen ZM. Position-Specific Differences in Speed Profiles Among National Football League Scouting Combine Participants. Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research. 2024.",
   "doi":"10.1519/jsc.0000000000004958",
   "abstract":"The study examined speed profiles (vmax, t and amax) calculated from the 40-yard dash and its splits in 2,156 NFL Scouting Combine participants across eight position groups. Skill positions had the greatest vmax and amax and lowest t, while offensive linemen had the lowest vmax and amax and highest t. Speed profile variables were related to other combine measures, with the weakest relationships found for offensive linemen.",
   "tldr":"Speed profile values from the NFL Combine differ clearly by position, with defensive backs and wide receivers fastest and offensive linemen slowest. Calculating vmax, t and amax can show a different picture than raw 40-yard dash times alone.",
   "methods":"Data came from NFL Scouting Combines in 2017, 2018, 2019, 2022 and 2023, with COVID-affected 2020 and 2021 excluded. Speed profile variables were calculated from 10-, 20- and 40-yard split times, and the author used one-way ANOVA and Pearson correlations with height, body mass, bench press, jump, pro-agility and L-cone tests.",
   "findings":"Defensive backs and wide receivers had the highest vmax (9.33 and 9.34 m/s) and amax and the lowest t, while offensive linemen had the lowest vmax (8.00 m/s) and amax (11.44 m/s/s) and the highest t (0.69 s). Across the whole sample, vmax, t and amax were significantly correlated with all combine tests, with the authors describing t as having the weakest relationships with the other combine measures.",
   "limitations":"The PDF prints the year 2024 (NSCA copyright and journal header), while the Crossref record lists 2025; the PDF year is used here. The text does not state the participants' sex, and it describes them only as NFL Scouting Combine (draft-prospect) participants, so the Professional / Elite tag is inferred from that event and matches the tag used for the same combine cohort in Q37. The paper is a secondary analysis of publicly available data with no direct measures of training or on-field speed. The author acknowledges that position-specific adaptation and genetic selection are not separated.",
   "practicalImplications":"Calculating vmax, t and amax from the 40-yard dash and splits can add information beyond raw times, and coaches can use position-level profiles to decide whether to prioritise maximal speed, acceleration or both. Because the study reports only group averages and correlations, these profiles should be compared with a player's own data rather than applied as fixed targets.",
   "athleteDev":"Performance Application: Speed profile values can guide position-specific sprint and acceleration priorities, and an individual athlete should be compared with his own profile over time. Offensive linemen and defensive linemen showed higher t and lower amax on average, so their acceleration work may need specific attention.",
   "rtp":"This study cannot inform return to sport. It contains no injured players, no return-to-play testing and no follow-up of sprint mechanics after injury, so the speed profiles should not be used to judge readiness to return."
 }
}

